//! member: true
//!
//! std.mem.budget — the region ledger, named, and the budget breach made
//! into a ROW. Stability tier: core.
//!
//! **What is the language's and what is std's** (F-0011, the sc02
//! structural finding, applied again). The language owns all of the
//! machinery here and owns it whole: `region r { … }` and `region()`
//! (`[mem.region.create.1]`), the byte ledger and its two builtin
//! queries `region_bytes(r)` / `live_region_bytes()`
//! (`[mem.region.account.1/.2]`, wolf-lang s131), the creation-time
//! budget `region r(cap: n) { … }` / `region(cap: n)` and the
//! deterministic `trap(alloc-contract)` a charge past it fires
//! (`[mem.region.cap.1/.2]`, s132/D68), and the containment that makes
//! that trap a value at a proc's join — `fault(kind)`, read as
//! `is_fault()` / `is_alloc_contract()` (`[mem.region.cap.3]`,
//! `[conc.proc.exit]`). std adds exactly two things over that, and
//! nothing else: **names for the two queries**, and **the containment
//! boilerplate collapsed into one call whose failure is an ordinary
//! error row a caller can handle**.
//!
//! What std deliberately does NOT add: a trap-shaped runner. Asking
//! "does this work fit in n bytes, and kill me if it does not" is
//! already `region r(cap: n) { … }` — three lanes, zero ceremony, no
//! library in the way. A wrapper would be a rename. What a caller
//! cannot write in one line is the CONTAINED form, and that is
//! `with_cap`.
//!
//! ## THE BUDGET IS DENOMINATED IN LEDGER UNITS, NOT PAYLOAD BYTES
//!
//! This is the sharpest thing on the page and it is the clause's own
//! sentence (`[mem.region.cap.1]`), restated here because a caller who
//! misses it will size a budget by arithmetic and be wrong by an order
//! of magnitude. The ledger is **per-tier**, **cumulative** and
//! **high-water**: a byte held as a container element charges the
//! ELEMENT's storage rather than one byte, and a grown container's
//! abandoned buffers stay charged for the region's whole life
//! (`[mem.region.account.1]`: "nothing is ever subtracted while the
//! region lives"). wolf-lang#203 measured the product on the io path —
//! a 64 KiB chunk read into a `List[int]` charges about **1 MiB**, 16x
//! the payload: 8x because a byte buffer is one machine int per byte,
//! 2x because the doubling growth's history stays charged. This
//! library's own measurement at the sc32 pin agrees to the byte and is
//! recorded in `docs/findings.md`.
//!
//! **THE 16x IS COLLECTED, AND THE CAVEAT SHRINKS TO A GROWTH HISTORY
//! (sc35).** The paragraph above is now history and is kept as history.
//! D72's `byte` (s135) retired the 8x element width; s136
//! (wolf-lang#231) moved the eight byte BUILTINS to `List[byte]`, and
//! sc35 renamed std's sixteen byte-tier signatures onto them. **Measured
//! at the io sites, through `std.fs.read_bytes`, at the sc35 pin: a
//! 64 KiB read charges 65,584 native and 65,536 checked — 1.00x on both
//! tiers, where it charged 1,048,560 / 1,048,576 (16.0x) at every pin
//! from sc32 to sc34.** Not 2.0x: a PRODUCER mints its list at exact
//! capacity through one memcpy and so has no growth history at all; the
//! 48 is one list header.
//!
//! **So the caveat a caller must still act on is not 16x — it is a 2x
//! GROWTH HISTORY on the native tier, and 1x on the checked one.** A
//! `List[byte]` a program GROWS by `push` to 65,536 elements still
//! charges 131,120 natively (`2 x payload + 48`, the doubling's
//! abandoned buffers, `[mem.region.account.1]`) and exactly 65,536 under
//! `--checked`, whose shadow model keeps no growth history. That
//! residue is #203's separable preallocation half, it is what
//! `reserve(n)` would retire, and no type change can touch it. **Read a
//! buffer with a producer and you pay the payload; build one by pushing
//! and you pay it twice on the tier that ships.**
//!
//! Two consequences a caller must act on:
//!
//! - **Derive budgets from measured `charged` readings, never from
//!   payload arithmetic or a per-tier constant.** A dry run of the same
//!   build pattern under no cap, `charged(r)` read at the end, is the
//!   portable way to a number — it is how wolf-lang's own three-tier
//!   cap witnesses pin the boundary on every tier at once.
//! - **The number is not comparable across lanes and must never be
//!   printed into a golden.** The same 100-element list measured at the
//!   sc32 pin reads 4064 under lupin, 2032 native and 1600 checked.
//!   Every witness in `tests/mem/budget/` therefore prints RELATIONS
//!   (zero at creation, monotone, stable, reclaimed) and never a count,
//!   which is what lets one `stdout=` hash cover every lane.
//!
//! And one gap the clause names rather than hides: on the native tier
//! `str` materialization's ambient region is the process root
//! (wolf-lang#191, the c09 seam), so string bytes appear in **no**
//! named region's ledger there today. Do not read the clause as "`str`
//! never charges" — it charges under lupin and the checked machine now,
//! and it will charge natively when the seam closes.
//!
//! ## NOTHING COMES BACK OUT OF `with_cap`, AND THAT IS MEASURED
//!
//! The obvious signature for this helper is `with_cap[T](n, f) -> T !
//! {exhausted}` — run the work, hand back what it made, or the row.
//! Every part of that was probed on all three lanes before a line of
//! this module was written, and it does not exist at this pin. Three
//! measurements, in the order they close the door:
//!
//! 1. A proc is a FAILURE DOMAIN with no shared address space
//!    (`[conc.proc.1]`: "nothing may assume shared address-space
//!    visibility into a proc beyond its channels"), so the only
//!    licensed way out is a channel.
//! 2. A `channel[int]` in a std function's SIGNATURE is `unsupported —
//!    methods on generic std data (the std surface)` on **both** wolf
//!    rungs (the F-0026 monomorphization family, reported at the caller's
//!    instantiation site).
//! 3. A `channel[int]` kept entirely inside a std body is `unsupported —
//!    methods without resolvable bodies` on the checked rung. It runs
//!    natively — but a returning `with_cap` would then be a
//!    lupin-and-native-only surface whose two-lane sibling is free.
//!
//! So the value stays inside. That is less of a loss than it reads,
//! because of what the contract already says: the capped region dies
//! with the proc (`[conc.proc.kill]` — regions bulk-free before the
//! reason delivers), so nothing it ALLOCATED could have survived the
//! call under any signature. Only a scalar was ever going to cross, and
//! this is the same discipline lobo's own request path already writes
//! by hand — the write rows leave its per-response region as an `int`
//! because heap values must not escape it. `with_cap` answers the one
//! question a scalar would have carried anyway: **did the work fit?**
//!
//! ## THE ROW IS A MARK, AND IT CARRIES NO NUMBER ON PURPOSE
//!
//! `exhausted` is lowercase and payload-free (API-CONVENTIONS §12: a
//! mark names a failure mode a caller can act on; CapCase is reserved
//! for a tag that carries a payload TYPE). The payload a reader will
//! reach for — "how much did it want?" — is not merely unavailable, it
//! is **unobservable by contract**: `[mem.region.cap.3]` makes teardown
//! free-then-deliver, so the breaching proc's regions are reclaimed
//! WHOLESALE before the reason reaches the join and no postmortem query
//! can see the dead proc's charge. A payload here would have to be
//! invented. The cap the caller passed is the caller's own value and
//! needs no return trip.
//!
//! ## Lanes (measured at the sc32 pin: wolf v0.2.2/8cda3aa, lupin
//! ## 0.1.22/pin 2bfbe5e)
//!
//! **The query half is three lanes.** `charged` and `live` run on
//! lupin, the checked rung and the native rung — including the shape
//! that is not obvious, a `region` passed ACROSS a module boundary.
//! Region values are affine (`[mem.region.create.2]`: they move, they
//! are never copied), so a reader is right to expect a call to consume
//! one; it does not, because the unwritten `read` mode is the spec's own
//! answer (`[mem.tier0.mode.read]`: the callee reads a value immutable
//! for the whole call, "the caller retains it"). Probed rather than
//! reasoned: two `charged(r)` calls in a row answer the same number on
//! every lane and the region is still usable after both.
//!
//! **The containment half is TWO lanes, and the missing one is not a
//! gap in this module.** `with_cap` is `unsupported — structured
//! concurrency in checked execution (C1 deferred)` on `wolf
//! conform-run --checked`, because D68's containment mechanism IS a
//! proc and the checked executor defers the whole task layer. lupin
//! 0.1.22 (is33) and the native rung both run it, byte-identically.
//! The refusal is at the tier, not at this surface: nothing this module
//! could be spelled differently to avoid it, short of not containing
//! the breach at all.
//!
//! **That refusal is reached at EXECUTION, not statically**, which is
//! why this module's three witnesses do not share one checked column.
//! `tests/mem/budget/negative_cap_trap.lu` calls the same `with_cap`
//! and is `run` on all three lanes, because its `assert` fires before
//! the `spawn proc` is ever reached; `breach_is_a_row.lu` reaches the
//! spawn and is refused. Probed apart from both, with a `with_cap` call
//! behind a runtime-false branch: the checked rung compiles that file
//! and runs it to `exit(0)`. A caller may therefore link this module on
//! the checked tier and only pay for the paths that actually spawn.
//!
//! **A region value may not be RETURNED from a function on the native
//! rung** — `unsupported — first-class region values beyond local
//! bindings (c05)` — which is why there is no `capped(n) -> region`
//! constructor here even though it is two lines and runs under lupin
//! and the checked rung. The value form `region(cap: n)` is the
//! language's, at the caller's own site, where it is three lanes.
//!
//! Regions (API-CONVENTIONS §5): no function here demands a region for
//! ordinary use. `charged` takes one because reading a specific
//! region's ledger is the whole operation — it is a query ABOUT a
//! region, not an allocation IN one — and `with_cap` creates and
//! destroys its own.

/// The bytes charged to `r`'s ledger — `[mem.region.account.1]`'s
/// cumulative count, named. O(1). Total: no error row; the trap
/// surface is the builtin's own (reading a stale region value faults
/// exactly where `in r` would).
///
/// The number's UNITS are an implementation-measured fact per tier and
/// are **not comparison surface** — see the module header. What every
/// tier guarantees, and what a program may rely on: zero at creation,
/// monotone within the region's lifetime, and stable between
/// allocations (two reads with no intervening charge agree).
///
/// `r` is read, not consumed: the region is usable after the call.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let r = region()
/// budget.charged(r) == 0
/// ```
pub fn charged(r: region) -> int {
    region_bytes(r)
}

/// The process-wide total the implementation holds for LIVE first-class
/// and sugar regions — `[mem.region.account.2]`, named. O(1). Total: no
/// error row, no traps.
///
/// It is the reclamation counter, **not an RSS proxy**: the process-root
/// arena is never counted, granularity is implementation-specified, and
/// a freed region's contribution vanishes wholesale at its free. On a
/// program with no live region it reads 0, and that is the clause
/// working rather than a broken gauge.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// budget.live() >= 0
/// ```
pub fn live() -> int {
    live_region_bytes()
}

/// Runs `f` under a region capped at `n` LEDGER UNITS, inside a proc, so
/// that a breach comes back as the row `exhausted` instead of ending the
/// process.
///
/// This is D68's containment made ergonomic. Without it a caller writes
/// the whole join by hand — spawn the proc, take its monitor, `select`
/// on the exit reason, ask `is_alloc_contract()` — for a question that
/// has one bit of answer. `[mem.region.cap.3]` is what makes the bit
/// trustworthy: the breaching proc dies by the killed-proc sequence, its
/// regions bulk-free BEFORE the reason delivers, and `live()` is already
/// back to its pre-call reading by the time this function returns. A
/// supervisor that answers 503 on this row was handed the memory back
/// first.
///
/// Nothing crosses back on the success path, by contract rather than by
/// omission — see the module header's third section. `f` runs in a
/// failure domain with no shared memory, and its region dies with it.
///
/// Traps `assert` when `n` is negative: a negative budget is not a
/// budget, it is a caller's arithmetic mistake, and API-CONVENTIONS §2
/// says such a thing traps rather than raising. The check is here, at
/// the door, rather than left to the language's own
/// `trap(alloc-contract)` at the creating site — that trap would fire
/// INSIDE the proc, be contained, and reach the caller as `exhausted`,
/// which would answer a bug with a recoverable row. `n == 0` is not a
/// mistake: it is a legal budget every charge breaches
/// (`[mem.region.cap.2]`), so `with_cap(0, f)` is the honest spelling
/// of "this work may allocate nothing".
///
/// Rows: `exhausted` and nothing else.
///
/// Lanes: lupin and the native rung. `wolf conform-run --checked`
/// refuses it — "structured concurrency in checked execution (C1
/// deferred)" — because the containment IS a proc. Measured at the sc32
/// pin; `tests/mem/budget/breach_is_a_row.lu` is the witness.
///
/// Example (prose — the fenced form cannot declare the `fn` this takes,
/// and a doc example is one synthesized `main`; the runnable form is
/// `tests/mem/budget/breach_is_a_row.lu`):
/// `budget.with_cap(4096, build) else |exhausted| { return 503 }` runs
/// `build` under a 4 KiB budget and routes the breach.
pub fn with_cap(n: int, f: fn()) -> () ! {exhausted} {
    if n < 0 {
        assert(false, "budget.with_cap: a negative byte budget is not a budget")
    }
    let w = spawn proc under_cap(n, f)
    let m = w.monitor()
    var ok = false
    select {
        exit(reason) from m => {
            ok = reason.is_normal()
        },
        timeout(30.s) => {
            ok = false
        },
    }
    if !ok {
        return exhausted
    }
}

// The proc's body. Private: `spawn proc` takes a CALL, so the work and
// its budget have to arrive as arguments to a named function rather
// than as a closure over them. The cap parenthesis follows the region's
// NAME (`[mem.region.cap.1]`'s sugar form) and `n` is evaluated once,
// at creation, before any allocation can charge (`[mem.region.cap.2]`).
//
// The `int` return is the proc's exit value and is never read: the join
// above asks the exit REASON, not the value, because the reason is
// where containment speaks. See the module header for why no value can
// cross this boundary.
fn under_cap(n: int, f: fn()) -> int {
    region tight(cap: n) {
        f()
    }
    0
}
